Identify Vulnerabilities

Before jumping into the vulnerabilities it is important to understand what a vulnerability actually is because it is easy to confuse it with an attack. A vulnerability is the weakness in the system, an attack is when someone actually exploits that weakness. Think of it like an unlocked window in your house the unlocked window is the vulnerability and someone climbing through it is the attack. What we are doing here is finding all the unlocked windows in Phoenix before anyone tries to climb through them.
For Phoenix this matters more than it would for a normal system because it operates during natural disasters, exactly when attackers are most active and the consequences of a security failure are most serious. The vulnerabilities below have been identified by going through every part of the Phoenix architecture and asking one simple question at each step what weakness here could an attacker exploit?

Vulnerability Category 1 — External Data Feed Vulnerabilities
These vulnerabilities are about the data coming into Phoenix from outside sources like BoM, Scamwatch and social media. This is the first place Phoenix connects to the outside world so any weakness here affects everything that comes after it.
V1 — No Continuous Check on Feed Content After Authentication
Phoenix checks that incoming feeds are coming from the right source using API keys and certificates but once that initial check passes it does not keep verifying whether the actual content has been tampered with. If an attacker managed to get inside a trusted data source and started changing the data being sent Phoenix would have no way of knowing because it already decided to trust that source. It is like checking someone's ID at the door and then never watching them again even if they start doing something suspicious.
V2 — No Detection of Unusual Patterns in Incoming Data
The validation component checks that incoming data looks right in terms of format and structure but it does not check whether the content actually makes sense compared to what has come before. If someone suddenly flooded the system with thousands of fake scam reports all at once the system would process them without raising any alarm. It is like a security guard who checks that your badge looks right but never notices that you are carrying something you should not be.
V3 — If One Data Source Goes Down There Is No Backup
If a primary external feed like BoM or Scamwatch goes offline during a disaster whether because of a technical failure or a deliberate attack — Phoenix has a retry mechanism but no automatic backup source to switch to. A sustained attack against one of these providers could leave Phoenix completely blind during exactly the period when it needs that information most.
V4 — Social Media APIs Can Be Forced to Cut Phoenix Off
Social media platforms like Twitter/X and Facebook limit how many requests any system can make within a certain time window. If an attacker deliberately floods these APIs with requests they can trick the platform into temporarily cutting off Phoenix's access creating a gap in monitoring for scams and misinformation right when social media activity is at its highest during a disaster.

Vulnerability Category 2 — Authentication and Access Vulnerabilities
These vulnerabilities are about who can get into Phoenix and what they can do once they are inside. This is one of the most critical areas because Phoenix's most powerful functions approving public alerts and configuring AI models are controlled by human operators whose accounts could be compromised.
V5 — DMT Staff Are More Vulnerable to Social Engineering During Disasters
Multi-factor authentication protects the login portal technically but it cannot protect the humans behind it from being tricked. When DMT staff are already under enormous pressure during a live disaster they are much more likely to fall for a convincing phishing email or respond to a fake urgent request than they would be on a normal day. Attackers know this and deliberately time their social engineering campaigns to coincide with disaster events. No technical fix fully solves this it requires ongoing training and clear communication protocols for high-pressure situations.
V6 — Wrong Permission Settings Could Give Someone Too Much Access
So RBAC is basically the system that decides who can do what inside Phoenix. the idea is that a Read-Only Analyst should only be able to view things, a DMT Reviewer can action things and an Administrator can change system settings. but if someone sets up those permissions incorrectly even just by accident during a routine update a lower level user could suddenly find themselves able to do things they were never supposed to do like approving public alerts or changing how the AI model works. in a system like Phoenix where those kinds of actions have real consequences for real people during a disaster that is not a small problem at all.
V7 — Session Tokens Can Be Stolen and Reused
so basically when someone logs into Phoenix successfully the system gives them a session token which is kind of like a digital pass that proves they are who they say they are. the problem is if that token gets stolen somewhere along the way an attacker can just use it to get straight into the system without needing the persons password or even going through MFA. its honestly like someone stealing your work ID card and just walking into the building with it like nothing happened.
V8 — Legitimate Users Can Cause Harm Without Triggering Any Alert
Role-Based Access Control governs what authenticated users are allowed to access but it does not monitor what they actually do within those permissions. A malicious insider who uses their own legitimate credentials to suppress genuine alerts, corrupt training data, or exfiltrate threat intelligence would not trigger any access control alarm because from the system's perspective they are doing exactly what they are permitted to do. Without behavioural monitoring on top of access control this vulnerability stays wide open.

Vulnerability Category 3 — Database and Storage Vulnerabilities
These vulnerabilities are about the Scam DB and Cyber Threat DB the two databases that store Phoenix's accumulated threat intelligence and feed the AI model. Because everything the AI model does depends on these databases any weakness here affects the entire system downstream.
V9 — Malicious Code Could Reach the Database Layer
Even though data passes through validation and preprocessing before being written to the databases if the sanitisation at the database write boundary is not thorough enough malicious content like SQL or NoSQL injection payloads could slip through and execute unintended commands in the database. Because both databases feed directly into the AI model's classifications and the Correlation Engine's risk scoring a successful injection attack here would corrupt the intelligence driving every decision Phoenix makes.
V10 — No Permanent Record of What Happened to the Data
If database operations reads, writes and deletes are not logged to an immutable audit trail then anyone who modifies or deletes records could do so without leaving any traceable evidence. This makes it almost impossible to detect slow quiet attacks like targeted record deletion or gradual data poisoning and makes recovery from any data integrity incident extremely difficult because you cannot establish what was changed and when.
V11 — Backups Could Be Wiped Out Along With the Primary Data
Phoenix's databases build up years worth of valuable threat intelligence over time. If the backup copies are stored in the same environment as the primary databases or protected by weaker access controls a ransomware attack could wipe out both the primary data and the backups simultaneously leaving no path to recovery without rebuilding everything from scratch which during an active disaster response would be catastrophic.
V12 — Fake Records Added Slowly Could Corrupt the AI Model
the AI model learns from the historical records stored in the Scam DB so if those records are not accurate the model is not going to be accurate either. the scary thing about this vulnerability is that it does not need to happen overnight. someone could just keep quietly adding fake but believable records over weeks or months and the model would slowly start learning the wrong things without anyone noticing until the detection accuracy has already dropped significantly and by that point fixing it is not easy.

Vulnerability Category 4 — AI Model Vulnerabilities
These vulnerabilities are specifically about the AI models that power Phoenix's threat detection. AI systems are powerful but they have known weaknesses that adversaries can deliberately exploit if they understand how the models work.
V13 — AI Models Can Be Fooled by Carefully Crafted Content
Phoenix's AI models are trained to classify content that resembles what they have seen before. But content that has been deliberately designed to sit just outside the model's decision boundaries called adversarial inputs can cause the model to produce confidently wrong classifications without any indication that something is wrong. This is a known limitation of all machine learning systems and cannot be completely eliminated. It can only be managed through continuous retraining, confidence threshold checks and human review of high-risk decisions.
V14 — Corrupted Feedback Could Make the AI Model Worse Over Time
The model retraining pipeline is designed to make Phoenix smarter after every disaster event. But if the feedback feeding into that pipeline is deliberately corrupted through fake community submissions, impersonation of official agencies or insider manipulation of review decisions the retraining process will actually make the model worse rather than better. This is one of the most dangerous vulnerabilities in Phoenix because it turns the improvement mechanism against itself and the degradation happens gradually enough to go unnoticed for a long time.
V15 — The AI Model Cannot Explain Its Own Decisions
Phoenix's deep learning models produce classifications without providing a human-readable explanation of how they reached that conclusion. This means DMT reviewers cannot easily tell why the model flagged a particular piece of content, cannot identify when the model is behaving unexpectedly and cannot explain decisions to external stakeholders who need to trust the system. In a high-stakes emergency context where accountability and public trust are critical the inability to explain AI decisions is a meaningful weakness.

Vulnerability Category 5 — Stream Processing Vulnerabilities
These vulnerabilities are about Apache Kafka and Firebase - the components responsible for routing data through the Phoenix pipeline in real time.
V16 — Kafka Broker Could Be Accessed by Unauthorised Users
Kafka is the backbone of Phoenix's data pipeline. If the Kafka broker is not properly secured for example if default credentials have not been changed, topic-level access controls are missing or the broker is reachable from outside the intended network an attacker who gets access can read, modify or inject messages across the entire pipeline simultaneously. Because every downstream component reads from Kafka a compromised broker is one of the most damaging vulnerabilities in the entire architecture.
V17 — Data Could Be Tampered With Between Pipeline Stages
Phoenix processes data step by step validation first then stream processing then preprocessing then the AI model. between each of those steps there is a tiny window where the data has already passed one check but has not reached the next one yet. in theory if someone had access to the pipeline at exactly the right moment they could change the data during that gap. it is not the easiest thing to pull off but the gap is real and worth being aware of.

Vulnerability Category 6 — Alert System Vulnerabilities
These vulnerabilities are about the final stage of Phoenix actually getting alerts out to communities, emergency services and government authorities.
V18 — Phoenix Cannot Control What Happens to Alerts After They Leave
Once an alert leaves Phoenix and enters an external delivery channel an SMS gateway, email server or VicEmergency Phoenix has no visibility over what happens to it. If any of those external channels is independently compromised the alert could be intercepted, modified or suppressed without Phoenix ever knowing. The system logs that it sent the alert but cannot verify that it arrived unchanged at the other end.
V19 — No Way to Confirm That Alerts Were Actually Received
Phoenix can confirm when it successfully sends an alert but it has no mechanism to verify in real time that the alert was actually received and read by the people it was intended for. During a disaster when communications infrastructure is already degraded an alert that appears successfully delivered on Phoenix's end might never have reached the community members who needed it most.

Vulnerability Category 7 — Community Feedback Vulnerabilities
These vulnerabilities are about the Community Feedback module the open channel through which community members and third-party responders can submit reports and provide feedback on alerts.
V20 — Anyone Can Submit Feedback and the Content Is Not Deeply Validated
The Community Feedback module is deliberately open to a wide range of users including the general public. While official agency submissions are weighted more heavily general community submissions are authenticated but not deeply validated for content accuracy. A determined adversary who submits plausible-looking but false feedback consistently over time could gradually influence the model retraining pipeline without any single submission being obviously suspicious enough to be filtered out.
V21 — The Quality Filter on Feedback Is Not Foolproof
The Discard if Irrelevant filter is designed to catch low-quality or adversarially crafted feedback before it enters the training pipeline. But the effectiveness of this filter depends on its own training data which means if the filter itself has been exposed to poisoned examples it may not catch adversarial submissions reliably. This creates a circular dependency that is difficult to fully resolve and leaves a residual vulnerability in the feedback and retraining pipeline.

