Why Threat Mapping Matters for Phoenix
So the whole point of threat mapping is that it turns three separate lists into an actual security strategy. without it you have attackers over here, attacks over there and vulnerabilities somewhere else but no clear picture of how they all connect. with it you can see straight away that Trust Boundary 2, the databases is the most heavily targeted part of the entire system, that V5 and V8 are the vulnerabilities being exploited the most and that Insider Threats and Organised Cybercrime Groups are responsible for the highest concentration of Critical severity threats. knowing all of that means the team can focus their energy on fixing the right things in the right places before the system goes live which is exactly what threat modelling is supposed to help you do.


